VPRINTF(3)                     P32W Unix                      VPRINTF(3)

NAME
       vprintf, vfprintf, vsprintf, vsnprintf - print a va_list

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdarg.h>

       int vprintf(const char *fmt, va_list ap);
       int vfprintf(FILE *f, const char *fmt, va_list ap);
       int vsprintf(char *s, const char *fmt, va_list ap);
       int vsnprintf(char *s, size_t n, const char *fmt, va_list ap);

DESCRIPTION
       These are printf, fprintf, sprintf and snprintf (printf(3))
       with the arguments in a va_list, started by va_start
       (stdarg(3)), instead of in the call. They let a program write
       its own printf-like functions. They do not call va_end; the
       caller does.

       All the printf family is one formatter: the conversions,
       flags and results are as printf(3) describes.

RETURN VALUE
       As for printf(3): the number of characters written (for
       vsnprintf, the number needed).

EXAMPLES
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdarg.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void fatal(const char *fmt, ...)
       {
           va_list ap;
           va_start(ap, fmt);
           fprintf(stderr, "prog: ");
           vfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);
           va_end(ap);
           fputc('\n', stderr);
           exit(1);
       }

SEE ALSO
       printf(3), stdarg(3)

BUGS
       As for printf(3). A va_list is a plain pointer here; after a
       v...printf call it has not moved, but portable programs
       should not count on that.
